.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt CHOWN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm chown ,
.Nm lchown ,
.Nm chown32 ,
.Nm lchown32 ,
.Nm fchown ,
.Nm fchown32 ,
.Nm fchownat
.Nd change file ownership
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn chown "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn lchown "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn fchown "int fd" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn fchownat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls change a file's owner and group.
An
.Fa owner
of
.Li (uid_t)-1
leaves the owner unchanged; a
.Fa group
of
.Li (gid_t)-1
leaves the group unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn chown
and
.Fn lchown
resolve
.Fa path
relative to the current working directory unless it is absolute.
.Fn chown
follows a final symbolic link on a file system that has them, and
.Fn lchown
changes the link itself, as on Linux.
The KRFFS boot root has no symbolic links, so there the two are the
same.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory: both calls follow a final symbolic link then, and
anything but a directory returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
The
.Pa /proc
links have fixed ownership, which
.Fn lchown
reaches.
.Fn chown
follows
.Pa /proc/self
to a process directory, whose ownership is fixed too, and does not
follow a
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd/ Ns Ar n
link, but it follows
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /exe
to the file at the program's path, as it follows a link of a mounted
type, and changes that file: a privileged
.Fn chown
of
.Pa /proc/self/exe
changes the program's owner on a writable file system.
.Fn fchown
uses the open descriptor
.Fa fd .
The kernel's
.Fn fchown
returns
.Er EBADF
for one opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
as Linux does, and for a pseudo-terminal slave descriptor with no live
terminal.
Musl's
.Fn fchown
retries that error, when
.Dv F_GETFD
finds the descriptor open, as a
.Fn chown
of
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
a link with fixed root ownership: a request of 0 or -1 for each ID
succeeds without a change, for any caller, and any other returns
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
.Fn fchownat
resolves a relative
.Fa path
from the directory descriptor
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
.Fa flags
may have two bits, and any other returns
.Er EINVAL
before the path is read.
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
changes a final symbolic link itself, as
.Fn lchown
does.
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
with an empty path changes the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to, one opened with
.Dv O_PATH
included, by the rules of
.Fn fchown ,
or the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An empty path without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
returns
.Er ENOENT ,
and a null one
.Er EFAULT .
.Ss Ownership rules
Changing the owner requires effective user ID 0.
Changing the group requires effective user ID 0, or ownership of the file
and membership in the requested group.
A request that changes neither field requires no ownership privilege.
Path resolution still requires search permission on ancestor directories.
.Pp
KRFFS and ext2 store ownership per file.
On a writable mounted KRFFS volume, an otherwise permitted change to its
root returns success without storing the IDs.
FAT32 and exFAT report the mount's owner and group; a change to either
is refused.
A pseudo-terminal slave under
.Pa /dev/pts
stores its own owner and group, but changing either requires effective
user ID 0.
Other synthetic objects under
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc ,
and other descriptor kinds, have fixed root ownership.
They accept only 0 or -1 for each ID.
A
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
node named by a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
follows the rules of its path.
On Linux a pipe or a socket has an owner of its own, which
.Fn fchown
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
change; here both have fixed root ownership.
.Pp
When either stored ID changes, set-user-ID is cleared on a nondirectory.
Set-group-ID is also cleared when group execute is set.
This applies even to a privileged caller.
Directories keep both bits; a file without group execute keeps
set-group-ID.
A request that changes neither ID changes neither mode nor timestamps.
.Ss System call numbers
The i386 numbers are 182 for
.Nm chown ,
16 for
.Nm lchown ,
212 for
.Nm chown32 ,
198 for
.Nm lchown32 ,
95 for
.Nm fchown ,
207 for
.Nm fchown32 ,
and 298 for
.Nm fchownat .
Each older number and its 32-bit variant use the same handler and
32-bit ID arguments.
Musl uses the 32-bit variants for
.Fn chown ,
.Fn lchown ,
and
.Fn fchown ;
it declares no separate functions with the
.Li 32
suffix.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/ownprobe.c
changes
.Pa /own.txt
to owner and group 1000, then changes its group to 2000 through the open
descriptor.
After dropping privilege, it checks that the owner can change the group
to supplementary group 3000 and that group 4000 is refused.
.Pp
.Pa tests/musl/lchownmusl.c
changes a file through
.Fn lchown
and each form of
.Fn fchownat ,
an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor and the working directory among them, and asks for each
refusal of the flags.
Given a directory on an ext2 volume, as
.Ic tests/run ext2
gives it
.Pa /tmp
on an ext2 root, it also requires that
.Fn lchown
and
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
change a symbolic link and leave its target alone, that
.Fn chown
changes the target, and that a dangling link can be changed.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission was denied on an ancestor directory.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open; a relative
.Fn fchownat
path uses an invalid or stale directory descriptor; or
.Fn fchownat
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
and an empty path was given a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open.
The kernel's
.Fn fchown ,
and
.Fn fchownat
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
also return it for a pseudo-terminal slave descriptor with no live
terminal, and
.Fn fchown
for a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH ;
musl's
.Fn fchown
retries the error through
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
as the description says.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
The path forms also return this error if there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn fchownat
was given a flag other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
or the file system cannot supply the required attributes.
.It Bq Er EIO
File system metadata could not be read or written, or the device was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path, its expansion relative to a directory, a component, or a
symbolic-link expansion is too long.
The copied path and its directory prefix must fit 4096 bytes,
including the terminating null byte.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
for
.Fn fchownat ,
or a required path component does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system could not allocate storage needed to resolve the path.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory,
.Fa dirfd
is not a directory descriptor for a relative path, or a path that ends
in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller lacks permission to make the ownership change, or the object
has fixed ownership.
.It Bq Er EROFS
A change requires an attribute write on a read-only mount or a file
system without an attribute-setting operation.
A request that changes neither ID does not perform that write.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
.Fn fchown
has no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
